---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CaseStudyHeader from "../../components/CaseStudyHeader.astro";
import CaseStudyTOC from "../../components/CaseStudyTOC.astro";
import ArchDiagram from "../../components/charts/ArchDiagram.astro";

const tocItems = [
  { id: "problem", label: "The problem" },
  { id: "architecture", label: "Architecture" },
  { id: "decisions", label: "Three core decisions" },
  { id: "state", label: "State and config" },
  { id: "production", label: "What it ran" },
  { id: "stack", label: "Stack" },
];

const stats = [
  { value: "10", label: "concurrent sessions" },
  { value: "100+", label: "devices managed" },
  { value: "2", label: "device pool types" },
  { value: "Frida", label: "runtime hooks" },
];

const flow = [
  { step: "1", name: "Producer", detail: "Internal automation request lands as a job on the SQS queue." },
  { step: "2", name: "Affinity check", detail: "Scheduler asks Postgres: is a similar workflow already running on a device? If yes, route there for warm-start." },
  { step: "3", name: "Pool select", detail: "If no warm device, choose between the on-prem Pixel rack (priority workloads) or cloud Genymotion pool (elastic capacity)." },
  { step: "4", name: "Runtime executor", detail: "Frida instrumentation engine, network recorder, automation runner (Appium / UIAutomator), and custom-script executor — all wired into one runtime." },
  { step: "5", name: "Capture", detail: "Execution logs, captured network logs, and automation results land in storage + internal analytics." },
];
---

<Base
  title="Hydra · Paritosh Tripathi"
  description="Hydra — a queue-driven orchestration platform for large-scale Android automation across hybrid on-prem and cloud device pools, with Frida runtime hooking and traffic capture."
>
  <CaseStudyHeader
    slug="hydra"
    title="Hydra"
    blurb="A control-plane platform for executing large-scale device-level automation across hybrid Android environments — physical Pixels on-prem and elastic Genymotion instances in the cloud, with affinity scheduling and runtime instrumentation."
    stats={stats}
  />

  <CaseStudyTOC items={tocItems} />
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 prose-invert">
    <h2 id="problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Mobile automation at scale was bottlenecked on three things: setting up devices was manual, jobs couldn't reuse warm devices for similar workflows, and instrumentation logic (Frida hooks, network capture) had to be baked into worker images at build time. Iteration was painful and capacity was lumpy.
    </p>
    <p>
      The platform replaces all three. Jobs go onto a queue. The scheduler finds a warm device when it can, falls back to a cloud Genymotion instance otherwise, and accepts Frida scripts as runtime input — no more redeploying workers to ship a new hook.
    </p>

    <h2 id="architecture">Architecture</h2>
    <ArchDiagram
      title="Hybrid pool · affinity scheduling · runtime instrumentation"
      caption="Affinity check before allocation is what cuts the warm-start cost. Frida scripts arrive as job input — no worker rebuild required to ship a new hook."
    >
      <svg viewBox="0 0 920 380" class="w-full h-auto" role="img" aria-label="Hydra architecture">
        <style>
          .d-node { fill: #1a1a1a; stroke: rgba(255,255,255,0.12); }
          .d-node-accent { fill: #1a1a1a; stroke: #ff8c00; }
          .d-pool-onprem { fill: #1a1a1a; stroke: rgba(80,227,194,0.4); }
          .d-pool-cloud { fill: #1a1a1a; stroke: rgba(74,144,226,0.4); }
          .d-store { fill: #121212; stroke: rgba(80,227,194,0.35); }
          .d-label { fill: #ededed; font: 500 12px "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, monospace; }
          .d-sub { fill: rgba(237,237,237,0.55); font: 400 10px "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, monospace; }
          .d-arrow { stroke: rgba(255,255,255,0.25); stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }
          .d-arrow-accent { stroke: #ff8c00; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }
        </style>
        <defs>
          <marker id="d-ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.4)"></path>
          </marker>
          <marker id="d-ah-saf" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="#ff8c00"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <rect x="20" y="30" width="140" height="50" rx="10" class="d-node" />
        <text x="90" y="52" text-anchor="middle" class="d-label">Producer</text>
        <text x="90" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="d-sub">automation request</text>

        <rect x="180" y="30" width="120" height="50" rx="10" class="d-node" />
        <text x="240" y="52" text-anchor="middle" class="d-label">SQS</text>
        <text x="240" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="d-sub">job queue</text>

        <rect x="320" y="20" width="200" height="80" rx="12" class="d-node-accent" />
        <text x="420" y="46" text-anchor="middle" class="d-label">Orchestrator</text>
        <text x="420" y="62" text-anchor="middle" class="d-sub">Python + Go · ECS</text>
        <text x="420" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="d-sub">scheduler · affinity engine</text>

        <rect x="540" y="30" width="170" height="50" rx="10" class="d-node-accent" />
        <text x="625" y="52" text-anchor="middle" class="d-label">Affinity check</text>
        <text x="625" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="d-sub">workflow already running?</text>

        <rect x="320" y="140" width="180" height="60" rx="10" class="d-pool-onprem" />
        <text x="410" y="166" text-anchor="middle" class="d-label">On-prem rack</text>
        <text x="410" y="184" text-anchor="middle" class="d-sub">always-on Pixel pool</text>

        <rect x="520" y="140" width="180" height="60" rx="10" class="d-pool-cloud" />
        <text x="610" y="166" text-anchor="middle" class="d-label">Cloud pool</text>
        <text x="610" y="184" text-anchor="middle" class="d-sub">Genymotion · elastic</text>

        <rect x="320" y="240" width="380" height="60" rx="10" class="d-node-accent" />
        <text x="510" y="266" text-anchor="middle" class="d-label">Runtime executor</text>
        <text x="510" y="282" text-anchor="middle" class="d-sub">Frida · network recorder · Appium · custom scripts</text>

        <rect x="40" y="260" width="200" height="55" rx="10" class="d-store" />
        <text x="140" y="286" text-anchor="middle" class="d-label">PostgreSQL · RDS</text>
        <text x="140" y="302" text-anchor="middle" class="d-sub">orchestration state</text>

        <rect x="40" y="320" width="200" height="50" rx="10" class="d-store" />
        <text x="140" y="342" text-anchor="middle" class="d-label">MongoDB</text>
        <text x="140" y="358" text-anchor="middle" class="d-sub">device + automation profiles</text>

        <rect x="320" y="320" width="380" height="50" rx="10" class="d-store" />
        <text x="510" y="342" text-anchor="middle" class="d-label">Storage · internal analytics</text>
        <text x="510" y="358" text-anchor="middle" class="d-sub">execution + network logs · automation results</text>

        <path class="d-arrow" marker-end="url(#d-ah)" d="M160 55 L180 55" />
        <path class="d-arrow" marker-end="url(#d-ah)" d="M300 55 L320 55" />
        <path class="d-arrow-accent" marker-end="url(#d-ah-saf)" d="M520 55 L540 55" />
        <path class="d-arrow" marker-end="url(#d-ah)" d="M420 100 L410 140" />
        <path class="d-arrow" marker-end="url(#d-ah)" d="M625 80 L610 140" />
        <path class="d-arrow" marker-end="url(#d-ah)" d="M410 200 L470 240" />
        <path class="d-arrow" marker-end="url(#d-ah)" d="M610 200 L550 240" />
        <path class="d-arrow" d="M420 100 L140 260" />
        <path class="d-arrow" d="M510 300 L510 320" />
      </svg>
    </ArchDiagram>

    <div class="my-8 rounded-xl border border-[var(--color-border)] bg-[var(--color-surface)] p-6 not-prose">
      <div class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-ink-faint)] mb-4">
        Stage by stage
      </div>
      <ol class="space-y-3">
        {
          flow.map((p) => (
            <li class="flex gap-4">
              <span class="font-mono text-xs text-[var(--color-saffron)] w-6 shrink-0 pt-1">{p.step}</span>
              <div>
                <div class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-ink)]">{p.name}</div>
                <div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-ink-muted)] leading-relaxed">{p.detail}</div>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>

    <h2 id="decisions">Three Core Decisions</h2>

    <h3>1. Hybrid pool — on-prem Pixels for priority, Genymotion in the cloud for elasticity</h3>
    <p>
      Pure cloud is elastic but slower to warm and more expensive per session for priority workloads. Pure on-prem is cheap but capped at the rack you bought. The hybrid pool gives priority workflows a reserved physical Pixel while letting bursty/elastic capacity ride on Genymotion images in AWS. The scheduler treats them as separate pools with different priority weights.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Affinity scheduling — route similar workflows to the same warm device</h3>
    <p>
      A cold Android session pays a meaningful setup cost — install / grant permissions / launch app / log in. If a similar automation is already running on a device, routing the new job to that same device skips the cold-start. The scheduler keeps a workflow signature in Postgres and matches incoming jobs against active sessions before allocating a new device.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Frida script injection at runtime</h3>
    <p>
      Hardcoding Frida hooks into worker images means every new instrumentation requirement is a worker rebuild + redeploy. Accepting Frida scripts as job input — one script per job, validated and sandboxed before injection — turned hours-long iteration into minutes. The cost is having to be careful about what scripts are allowed; that's a smaller surface to defend than redeploying the fleet.
    </p>

    <h2 id="state">State and config</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>PostgreSQL (RDS)</strong> — job scheduling, device allocation, execution tracking, automation lifecycle</li>
      <li><strong>MongoDB</strong> — device capabilities, application configs, automation profiles (config-driven execution)</li>
      <li><strong>Internal dashboard</strong> — health checks and device availability surfaced to operators in real time</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="production">What it ran</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>10 concurrent</strong> active automation sessions sustained</li>
      <li>Infrastructure capable of managing <strong>100+ connected devices</strong> across physical and virtual pools</li>
      <li>Frida instrumentation, network recording, and Appium / UIAutomator unified under one execution layer</li>
      <li>Captured execution logs, network traffic, and automation results piped to internal analytics</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="stack">Stack</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Python + Go control plane on AWS ECS</li>
      <li>AWS SQS for job queue</li>
      <li>PostgreSQL (RDS) for orchestration state, MongoDB for configs / profiles</li>
      <li>Frida, Appium, UIAutomator for runtime instrumentation and automation</li>
      <li>Genymotion for cloud Android instances; on-prem Pixel device rack for priority workloads</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
